<article class="essay">

<section class="mv" id="m1" data-t="Hold, heart">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">I</div>
  <blockquote class="greek-quote">
    <p lang="grc" class="grc"><span class="ln" data-fade style="--d:0">στῆθος δὲ πλήξας κραδίην ἠνίπαπε μύθῳ·</span><br /><span class="ln" data-fade style="--d:1">τέτλαθι δή, κραδίη· καὶ κύντερον ἄλλο ποτ᾽ ἔτλης.</span></p>
    <p class="translation" data-fade style="--d:2">He struck his chest and called his heart to order: “Hold, heart. You have held through worse than this.”</p>
    <cite data-fade style="--d:3">Homer, <em>Odyssey</em> 20.17–18</cite>
  </blockquote>
  <h2 data-fade><span>I.</span> Hold, heart</h2>
  <p data-fade><span class="tag text" data-tip="Homer, Odyssey 20.17–18">text</span> Odysseus is lying awake in his own hall, disguised as a beggar, listening to the suitors’ women laugh on their way to the suitors’ beds. Something in him wants to get up and kill them all tonight. Something else knows that tomorrow is the day, and that tonight is for lying still. The poet does not say “he restrained himself.” He says Odysseus struck his own chest and spoke to his heart as to a dog that needs settling. The heart, <span lang="grc">κραδίη</span>, is addressed in the second person. It has a history of its own (“you have held through worse”) and it can be reasoned with.</p>
  <p data-fade><span class="tag text" data-tip="Plato, Republic 441a–b">text</span> That is the oldest surviving record in Greek of a person discovering that he is more than one. The discovery is older than philosophy by three centuries, and philosophy knew it. When Plato needs to prove that the part of us that reasons is a different part from the part that surges, he does not build an argument first. He quotes this line (<em>Republic</em> 441b). Homer had already done the experiment.</p>
</section>

<section class="mv" id="m2" data-t="The thirsty man and the man at the wall">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">II</div>
  <h2><span>II.</span> The thirsty man and the man at the wall</h2>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Plato, Republic IV 436b–c; 439a–d">text</span> Plato’s argument, when it comes, is short and has never been improved on. The same thing cannot do or undergo opposites in the same respect at the same time (436b). A thirsty man who stands at the water and does not drink is therefore two things: a thing that reaches for the cup and a thing that holds the hand back (439c). He calls the reaching thing <span lang="grc">τὸ ἐπιθυμητικόν</span>, the desiring part, and the holding thing <span lang="grc">τὸ λογιστικόν</span>, the reckoning part, from <span lang="grc">λογίζομαι</span>, to count, to calculate, to take stock.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. 439e–440a">text</span> Then he tells a story to find the third. Leontius, son of Aglaion, is walking up from the Piraeus when he notices corpses lying at the executioner’s ground outside the north wall. He wants to look. He is disgusted that he wants to look. He fights himself, covers his eyes, loses, and runs up to the bodies with his eyes forced open, shouting at them: <span lang="grc" class="veil">ἰδοὺ ὑμῖν, ὦ κακοδαίμονες, ἐμπλήσθητε τοῦ καλοῦ θεάματος</span>, “There, you wretches, drink your fill of the lovely sight” (439e–440a).</p>

  <div class="play look" id="lookplay" data-label="Rep. 439e–440a · outside the north wall">
    <svg class="wall" viewBox="0 0 600 200" aria-hidden="true">
      <rect x="0" y="88" width="600" height="38" fill="#121521" stroke="#9a958a" stroke-opacity=".35"/>
      <path d="M0 107H600M60 88v19M120 107v19M180 107v19M240 88v19M300 88v19M360 107v19M420 107v19M480 88v19M540 88v19" stroke="#9a958a" stroke-opacity=".22" fill="none"/>
      <path d="M0 172H600" stroke="#9a958a" stroke-opacity=".4"/>
      <g class="leon">
        <path d="M200 82v18M188 100h24M200 100l-6 40M200 100l6 40M194 140l-8 32M206 140l8 32" stroke="#e6dfcf" stroke-opacity=".7" fill="none" stroke-width="1"/>
        <circle cx="200" cy="72" r="9" fill="none" stroke="#e6dfcf" stroke-width="1"/>
        <g fill="#e6dfcf"><circle cx="200" cy="72" r="2"/><circle cx="188" cy="100" r="2"/><circle cx="212" cy="100" r="2"/><circle cx="194" cy="140" r="2"/><circle cx="206" cy="140" r="2"/><circle cx="186" cy="172" r="2"/><circle cx="214" cy="172" r="2"/></g>
        <g class="arm l" stroke="#e8c877" fill="#e8c877" stroke-width="1.2"><path d="M188 100l8-28" fill="none"/><circle cx="196" cy="72" r="3"/></g>
        <g class="arm r" stroke="#e8c877" fill="#e8c877" stroke-width="1.2"><path d="M212 100l-8-28" fill="none"/><circle cx="204" cy="72" r="3"/></g>
        <path class="gaze" d="M212 72L560 150" stroke="#e8c877" stroke-opacity=".6" stroke-dasharray="2 6" fill="none"/>
      </g>
      <text class="lab" x="12" y="190">up from the Piraeus</text>
      <text class="lab" x="588" y="190" text-anchor="end">the executioner’s ground, out of frame &rarr;</text>
    </svg>
    <p lang="grc" class="grc big veil">ἰδοὺ ὑμῖν, ὦ κακοδαίμονες, ἐμπλήσθητε τοῦ καλοῦ θεάματος</p>
    <p class="translation seenonly">“There, you wretches, drink your fill of the lovely sight.”</p>
    <div class="row"><button class="btn hold" id="lookbtn" aria-pressed="false">Press and hold to look</button><button class="btn ghost" id="lookreset" hidden>Cover his eyes again</button><div class="out" id="lookout">His hands are over his eyes. The bodies are off to the right, out of the frame, and they stay there.</div></div>
  </div>

  <p>Plato’s point is the shouting. The thing in Leontius that is angry is angry <em>at his own eyes</em>. It is on the side of the reasoning part, and it has teeth. He names it <span lang="grc">τὸ θυμοειδές</span>, the thumos-like part, and his whole account of the soul turns on the fact that there are three, since anger at oneself is neither appetite nor calculation.</p>
  <p><span class="tag read" data-tip="Chantraine, Dictionnaire étymologique, s.v. θυμός, θύω">reading</span> The Greek words are doing more than the English lets through. <span lang="grc">θυμός</span>, the surge in the chest, the seat of anger and pride and courage in Homer, sits inside <span lang="grc">ἐπιθυμία</span>: appetite is literally a thumos-<em>toward</em>, a surge aimed at an object. The two lower parts share a root, and the root is heat; the family of <span lang="grc">θύω</span>, to rush and to smoke, is cousin to Latin <em>fumus</em>. Reason, by contrast, is named for a bookkeeper’s verb. Plato’s soul is two fires and an accountant.</p>
</section>

<section class="mv" id="m3" data-t="Vienna, 1923">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">III</div>
  <h2><span>III.</span> Vienna, 1923: the I and the It</h2>
  <div class="vienna" role="img" aria-label="Das Ich, das Es, das Über-Ich, set large, with ego, id and super-ego laid thinly over them">
    <div class="de" style="--d:0"><span class="w" lang="de">Ich</span><span class="la" lang="la">ego</span></div>
    <div class="de" style="--d:1"><span class="w" lang="de">Es</span><span class="la" lang="la">id</span></div>
    <div class="de" style="--d:2"><span class="w" lang="de">Über-Ich</span><span class="la" lang="la">super-ego</span></div>
  </div>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Freud, Das Ich und das Es (1923), chs. II–III; Nietzsche, Jenseits von Gut und Böse §17">text</span> Twenty-three centuries later a physician in Vienna publishes a short book called <em>Das Ich und das Es</em>. The title is two pronouns: the I and the It. The third term, introduced in chapter III, is <em>das Über-Ich</em>, the Over-I. Freud chose the plainest words German has. He took <em>das Es</em> from Georg Groddeck, whose <em>Book of the It</em> appeared the same year, and he notes in a footnote that Groddeck took the usage from Nietzsche, who had written in <em>Beyond Good and Evil</em> (§17) that a thought comes when <em>it</em> wants, not when <em>I</em> want: <em>es denkt</em>. The It is the grammar of everything that happens in you without your having done it. “It came over me.” “It got the better of me.” Every language already had the word; Freud only capitalized it.</p>
  <p><span class="tag read" data-tip="Bettelheim, Freud and Man’s Soul (1983)">reading</span> His English translators, from Joan Riviere’s 1927 version to James Strachey’s Standard Edition, buried the pronouns under Latin: ego, id, super-ego. Bruno Bettelheim spent a book (<em>Freud and Man’s Soul</em>, 1983) on what that cost. Freud’s <em>Seele</em>, soul, became “mind” and “mental apparatus.” The Over-I, which any German child could parse, became a technical term. And a plain-spoken, three-part account of the soul that a reader of the <em>Republic</em> would have recognized on sight was dressed in enough Latin that the recognition rarely happened. The Latin made Freud sound like a scientist. It also made him sound as though he had no ancestors.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Freud, Drei Abhandlungen, preface to the 4th ed. (1920)">text</span> He knew he had them. In 1920 he wrote that psychoanalysis’s enlarged idea of sexuality “coincides with the Eros of the divine Plato.” Eros, Oedipus, Narcissus: every name he reached for when he needed one was Greek. The three-part soul he did not name, and the resemblance has to be read out.</p>
</section>

<section class="mv" id="m4" data-t="The lineup">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">IV</div>
  <h2><span>IV.</span> The lineup</h2>
  <p>Set the two triads side by side and the fit is close at the bottom, loose in the middle, and startling at the top.</p>

  <div class="play lineup" id="lineup" data-label="Three triads · one name layer">
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="col plato">
        <h3>Plato <small>Republic IV, c. 375 BC</small></h3>
        <ol>
          <li><span class="term" lang="grc">τὸ θυμοειδές</span><span class="g">the thumos-like part</span></li>
          <li><span class="term" lang="grc">τὸ λογιστικόν</span><span class="g">the reckoning part</span></li>
          <li><span class="term" lang="grc">τὸ ἐπιθυμητικόν</span><span class="g">the desiring part</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="col freud" data-state="latin">
        <h3>Freud <small>Das Ich und das Es, 1923</small></h3>
        <ol>
          <li><span class="term" lang="de">das Über-Ich</span><span class="g">the Over-I</span><span class="over la" lang="la">super-ego</span><span class="over gr" lang="grc">θυμοειδές</span></li>
          <li><span class="term" lang="de">das Ich</span><span class="g">the I</span><span class="over la" lang="la">ego</span><span class="over gr" lang="grc">λογιστικόν</span></li>
          <li><span class="term" lang="de">das Es</span><span class="g">the It</span><span class="over la" lang="la">id</span><span class="over gr" lang="grc">ἐπιθυμητικόν</span></li>
        </ol>
        <span class="sheet-tab" aria-hidden="true">a layer &middot; tap to lift</span>
      </div>
      <div class="col berne">
        <h3>Berne <small>Games People Play, 1964</small></h3>
        <ol>
          <li><span class="term">Parent</span><span class="g">the trained part</span></li>
          <li><span class="term">Adult</span><span class="g">the one doing the reckoning</span></li>
          <li><span class="term">Child</span><span class="g">the surge</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="row"><button class="btn" id="layerbtn">Lift the Latin</button><div class="out" id="layerout"></div></div>
  </div>

  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. IX 580e–581a">text</span> <strong>The It and the desiring part.</strong> These are nearly the same word. Plato’s <span lang="grc">ἐπιθυμητικόν</span> is “the desiring thing”; Freud’s <em>Es</em> is the reservoir of the <em>Triebe</em>, the drives. Plato adds that it is also the money-loving part, since money is how desires get fed (580e–581a), and Freud would have had no quarrel with that. Both describe it as the largest part, the oldest, and the one that does not know what time it is.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. IX 581b–582e; Freud, Neue Folge (1933), Lecture 31; Das Ich und das Es, ch. II; Phaedrus 246a, 253d–e">text</span> <strong>The I and the reckoning part.</strong> Here the fit slips. Plato’s <span lang="grc">λογιστικόν</span> has a desire of its own: it loves learning and truth, it is <span lang="grc">φιλόσοφον</span>, and it has a pleasure proper to it that the other parts cannot taste (581b–582e). Freud’s I has no appetite. In the 1933 lectures he calls it the servant of three harsh masters, the outer world, the It, and the Over-I, and its only native talent is reality-testing: knowing what is actually there. Freud’s I is Plato’s reckoning part with the love of truth removed and the bookkeeping left in. His image for it is a rider who must steer a stronger horse, and who often, to stay in the saddle, has to guide it where it already wanted to go (<em>Das Ich und das Es</em>, ch. II). Plato’s charioteer in the <em>Phaedrus</em> (246a, 253d–e) has two horses and a whip, and the whole point is that the good horse pulls with him.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. 441a, 441e–442a; Freud, Das Unbehagen in der Kultur (1930), ch. VII; Das Ich und das Es, ch. III">text</span> <strong>The Over-I and the thumos part.</strong> This is the match that makes the essay. Both are the part that turns on you. Leontius’s rage at his own eyes is a shame reaction; Freud’s Over-I is the seat of shame’s sibling, guilt, and its severity, he says, is borrowed aggression, the It’s own violence turned inward. Both are the <em>trained</em> part. Plato says thumos is reason’s natural ally “unless corrupted by bad upbringing” (441a), and that music and gymnastics exist to tune it (441e–442a). Freud says the Over-I is built out of the parents’ voices and the culture’s demands, the “heir of the Oedipus complex.” Both say the trained part can be trained wrong, and that when it is, it stops being an ally and becomes a jailer. Plato’s word for the jailer version is a soul ruled by <span lang="grc">φιλονικία</span>, love of winning, the timocratic man of Book VIII. Freud’s is melancholia.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. IX 581c">text</span> The difference underneath is this. Plato’s three parts each have their own <em>eros</em> and their own object: truth, honor, gain (581c). The soul is a small city with three estates and three economies. Freud’s three parts are stations for one energy, libido, which moves among them like water through locks. Plato wrote a politics of the soul. Freud wrote its hydraulics.</p>
</section>

<section class="mv" id="m5" data-t="Where It was">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">V</div>
  <h2><span>V.</span> Where It was</h2>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Freud, Neue Folge der Vorlesungen (1933), Lecture 31">text</span> The hydraulics show in Freud’s most famous sentence, the last line of Lecture 31: <em lang="de">Wo Es war, soll Ich werden.</em> Where It was, I shall come to be. Strachey gave it as “Where id was, there ego shall be,” which is accurate and sounds like a motto on a building. Freud’s own gloss is the one that matters: it is a work of culture, he says, like the draining of the Zuider Zee. The It is a sea. The I is reclaimed land. Progress is dikes.</p>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Rep. 433a–b, 443d">text</span> Plato ends his account of the soul in the same place with a different instrument. Justice, he says, is each part doing its own work (433a–b), reason ruling, thumos in alliance, desire ruled. But when he describes what that rule <em>feels like</em> from inside, the image is musical: the just man “binds the three together like the three fixed notes of a scale, lowest, highest, and middle,” <span lang="grc">νεάτης τε καὶ ὑπάτης καὶ μέσης</span>, “and becomes one out of many” (443d). Freud drains a sea. Plato tunes a lyre. Both say the I should rule; they disagree about what ruling looks like. For Freud it looks like land where there was water. For Plato it looks like consonance, three strings that were already there, brought into one sound.</p>

  <div class="play instruments" id="instruments" data-label="Two instruments · Lecture 31 and Rep. 443d" data-og-frame data-twin="Two instruments, Lecture 31 and Republic 443d: a three-string lyre, νεάτη, μέση and ὑπάτη, whose pegs the reader tunes until the beating stops, Plato's image for a just soul; and the Zuider Zee, which the reader drains by dragging right, Freud's image for where It was. The reader chooses which of the two the section closes on.">
