      </svg>
      <ul class="figkey">
        <li class="k-man"><b>the man within</b><span>reason &middot; <span class="tri" data-part="mid"><span class="t-la" lang="la">ego</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Ich</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">λογιστικόν</span></span></span><span class="g">faint, small, last to arrive, and pulsing</span></li>
        <li class="k-lion"><b>the lion</b><span>thumos &middot; <span class="tri" data-part="top"><span class="t-la" lang="la">super-ego</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Über-Ich</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">θυμοειδές</span></span></span><span class="g">the part with teeth, trainable either way</span></li>
        <li class="k-beast"><b>the many-headed beast</b><span>appetite &middot; <span class="tri" data-part="low"><span class="t-la" lang="la">id</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Es</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">ἐπιθυμητικόν</span></span></span><span class="g">the largest, the oldest, the one fed first</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Romans 7:15, 7:22–23; Exodus 20:17 (LXX); Galatians 5:17">text</span> Four centuries later Paul writes to Rome: “I do not do the good I want; the evil I do not want, that I do” (Romans 7:15). “I delight in the law of God <span lang="grc">κατὰ τὸν ἔσω ἄνθρωπον</span>, according to the inner man, but I see another law in my members at war with the law of my mind” (7:22–23). The inner man is Plato’s phrase with one preposition swapped. The war in the members is Leontius at the wall. And the Greek of the Tenth Commandment, as the Septuagint has it, legislates on Plato’s lowest part by name: <span lang="grc">οὐκ ἐπιθυμήσεις</span>, you shall not <em>desire</em> (Exodus 20:17). Paul says the same in Galatians with the verb intact: the flesh <span lang="grc">ἐπιθυμεῖ</span> against the spirit (5:17).</p>

  <div class="play inner" id="inner" data-label="589a · Romans 7:22 · one preposition">
    <div class="grid" lang="grc">
      <span class="who" lang="en">Plato</span><span class="w plato">ὁ</span><span class="w plato prep">ἐντὸς</span><span class="w plato">ἄνθρωπος</span>
      <span class="who" lang="en">Paul</span><span class="w paul">ὁ</span><span class="w paul prep">ἔσω</span><span class="w paul">ἄνθρωπος</span>
    </div>
    <p class="out">The article and the noun are the same letters. Only the preposition changed: <span lang="grc">ἐντός</span>, within, for <span lang="grc">ἔσω</span>, inside. Both phrases in the nominative.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Read laterally, the three corpora, the philosopher, the apostle, and the physician, are one conversation about one discovery, carried on for two and a half thousand years in two languages, and the discovery is Homer’s: the thing you call “I” is the smallest of the three, it was last to arrive, and its first act was to speak to the others.</p>
</section>

<section class="mv" id="m7" data-t="Parent, Adult, Child">
  <div class="wm" aria-hidden="true">VII</div>
  <h2><span>VII.</span> Parent, Adult, Child</h2>
  <p><span class="tag text" data-tip="Berne, Games People Play (1964), ch. 2">text</span> The conversation did not stop in Vienna. In 1964 Eric Berne published <em>Games People Play</em> and gave the triad its third set of names, in a language even plainer than Freud’s German: Parent, Adult, Child. Berne insisted these were not the Over-I, the I, and the It, that they were observable states a person visibly shifts between in a room, and he was right that the method was different. But the shape is the shape. The Parent is the trained part, the recorded voices. The Child is the surge. The Adult is the one doing the reckoning, and Berne’s whole therapy is Plato’s: get the Adult into the chair.</p>
  <div class="pac" role="list" aria-label="Parent, Adult, Child, each with the earlier name">
    <div class="pc" role="listitem"><b>Parent</b><span class="g">the trained part, the recorded voices</span><span class="tri" data-part="top"><span class="t-la" lang="la">super-ego</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Über-Ich</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">θυμοειδές</span></span></div>
    <div class="pc" role="listitem"><b>Adult</b><span class="g">the one doing the reckoning</span><span class="tri" data-part="mid"><span class="t-la" lang="la">ego</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Ich</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">λογιστικόν</span></span></div>
    <div class="pc" role="listitem"><b>Child</b><span class="g">the surge</span><span class="tri" data-part="low"><span class="t-la" lang="la">id</span><span class="t-de" lang="de">das Es</span><span class="t-gr" lang="grc">ἐπιθυμητικόν</span></span></div>
  </div>
  <p>What Berne added was something neither Plato nor Freud had: the observation that two people’s triads play <em>each other</em>. The Parent in me hooks the Child in you, and the two of us run a script neither of us wrote. That is a Greek idea too, though Berne did not say so. It is what the tragedians meant when they made the Furies a family inheritance.</p>
  <p class="disputed"><span class="tag disp" data-tip="The author’s own argument, forward-looking; the bridge to the Futurist series">disputed</span> And the lens turns forward. The three parts now have prosthetics. The feed is the desiring part’s broker, tuned to its heat by machines that measure where the eyes go, which is to say it is Leontius’s executioner’s ground, optimized. The metrics, the counts, the public ledger of likes and views, are an Over-I that lives outside the skull and never sleeps. The reckoning part, the small man inside, is the only one of the three that no one has built an engine for yet, and it is the one being squeezed. That is a separate essay, in a drier series. But it starts here, with the Greek, because the Greek is where we first learned that there were three of us in the chest, and that the one who speaks last is the one who has to speak first.</p>

  <a class="companion" href="/gallery/everyone-searchable/">
    <div class="eyebrow">The separate essay, in the drier series &middot; Futurist &middot; No. 1</div>
    <div class="ct">Everyone, Searchable</div>
    <p>Every human life is about to become searchable through a god-level AI. What it does to the searcher, the searched, and the one move that holds.</p>
    <div class="mono">Open Everyone, Searchable &rarr;</div>
  </a>

